    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assertion $(1)$ follows immediately from Proposition <a href="/tag/47329B" data-tag="47329B">5.3.5.10</a>. If $\calC \in \widehat{\Cat }_{\infty }$ belongs to the essential image of $\theta $, then $\calC $ is essentially small and idempotent complete (because $\calC $ is stable under retracts in an idempotent complete $\infty $-category). Conversely, suppose that $\calC $ is essentially small and idempotent complete, and choose a minimal model $\calC ' \subseteq \calC $. Then $\Ind _{\kappa }(\calC ')$ is $\kappa $-accessible. Moreover, the collection of $\kappa $-compact objects of $\Ind _{\kappa }(\calC ')$ is an idempotent completion of $\calC '$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/F36966" data-tag="F36966">5.4.2.4</a>), and therefore equivalent to $\calC $ (since $\calC '$ is already idempotent complete). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
